  <article class="blog-post">
    <header class="blog-header">
      <p class="blog-date">April 20, 2026</p>
      <h1>Your Passport Photo Is for Sale on Telegram — Why Centralized KYC Is a Honeypot</h1>
      <p class="blog-subtitle">
        Every time you scan your ID for a KYC check, that image is stored on someone else's server.
        When that server gets breached — and it will — your identity is sold on Telegram for pennies.
        There's a better way.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The breach that nobody talks about</h2>

      <p>
        In October 2024, crypto on-ramp provider <strong>Transak</strong> disclosed a security incident.
        An attacker gained access to an employee's laptop through a phishing attack, then used those
        credentials to log into the dashboard of a third-party KYC vendor. The data accessed included
        names, dates of birth, and — most critically — <strong>ID documents: passport scans and
        driver's license photos.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Transak's response emphasized that "no financially sensitive information" was compromised.
        But here's the thing: <strong>your passport photo is more valuable than your credit card number.</strong>
        You can cancel a credit card in five minutes. You cannot cancel your face, your date of birth,
        or your national ID number. That data is permanent, and once it's in the wild, it's used to
        open bank accounts, apply for loans, and create synthetic identities — for years.
      </p>

      <p class="blog-video-link">
        Watch the full investigation into Telegram's role in this ecosystem:
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_-7nXVpJsRo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          ARTE — "Telegram: The App Scammers Love"
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The Telegram marketplace</h2>

      <p>
        The ARTE documentary exposes how Telegram has become the preferred marketplace for stolen
        personal data. Channels with thousands of subscribers post "logs" — structured dumps of
        stolen user data including:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Passwords and autofill data harvested by infostealer malware</li>
        <li>Browser cookies (which bypass two-factor authentication entirely)</li>
        <li>Credit card numbers</li>
        <li>Crypto wallet credentials</li>
        <li>And increasingly: <strong>KYC identity documents</strong> — passport photos, national ID
        scans, selfies with documents held next to faces</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The documentary shows French national identity cards (<em>Carte Nationale d'Identite</em>)
        being traded openly on Telegram, sourced from breached KYC providers. These aren't stolen
        from individuals — they're stolen from the <em>companies that asked individuals to upload
        their documents.</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        The pattern is consistent: a fintech, crypto exchange, or online service requires KYC
        verification. The user uploads their passport scan to a third-party verification provider.
        That provider stores the image — often unencrypted or with inadequate access controls — in
        a centralized database. An attacker compromises one employee account, and suddenly has access
        to thousands or millions of identity documents.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The architectural problem</h2>

      <p>
        This is not a security failure that can be fixed with better firewalls or more employee
        training. <strong>The architecture itself is the vulnerability.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        The current KYC model creates what security researchers call a <em>honeypot</em>: a single
        location containing high-value data for millions of users. The economics are simple — an
        attacker only needs to breach one system to get everything. And the defender must be perfect
        every day, while the attacker only needs to succeed once.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>What gets stored</th>
            <th>Why it's dangerous</th>
            <th>Can the user revoke it?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Passport photo</td>
            <td>Identity theft, synthetic identity creation</td>
            <td>No — your face doesn't change</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>National ID number</td>
            <td>Opens bank accounts, credit lines, government services</td>
            <td>No — assigned for life in most countries</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Date of birth</td>
            <td>Combined with name, unlocks most security questions</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Selfie with document</td>
            <td>Bypasses liveness checks on other platforms</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Credit card number</td>
            <td>Fraud</td>
            <td><strong>Yes</strong> — cancel and reissue in minutes</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Notice the irony: the only piece of data the user can actually revoke (the credit card) is
        the one Transak emphasized was <em>not</em> compromised. Everything that <em>was</em>
        compromised — names, dates of birth, ID documents — is permanent and irrevocable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How Verifiable Credentials eliminate the honeypot</h2>

      <p>
        The fundamental question is: <strong>why does a KYC provider need to store your passport
        photo at all?</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        In the current model, they store it because that's the only way to prove, after the fact,
        that they verified your identity. The image is the evidence. But this conflates two distinct
        operations: <em>verification</em> (checking that you are who you claim to be) and
        <em>storage</em> (keeping the evidence indefinitely).
      </p>

      <p>
        With Verifiable Credentials, these operations are separated:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Verification happens once.</strong> The user presents a government-issued
          identity credential (a VC signed by the national authority's DID). The verifier checks
          the cryptographic signature — is this credential real, current, and issued by a trusted
          authority? This takes milliseconds and requires no image storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The proof is the signature, not the image.</strong> The verifier stores a record
          that says: "On this date, we verified a credential of type NationalID, issued by
          did:web:registro-civil.go.cr, for subject did:sns:user.attestto.sol. Signature valid."
          No passport photo. No ID number. Just a cryptographic receipt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The original credential stays on the user's device.</strong> It never leaves
          their encrypted vault. There is no centralized database of identity documents to breach,
          because no such database exists.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        The attacker's equation changes completely. Instead of breaching one KYC vendor to get
        millions of identity documents, they would need to breach millions of individual devices —
        each protected by its own encryption keys and biometric unlock. The honeypot disappears.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Selective disclosure: proving without revealing</h2>

      <p>
        But what about services that need to verify specific attributes? A bar needs to verify you're
        over 21 — but doesn't need your passport number, address, or photo. An employer needs to
        verify you're authorized to work — but doesn't need your date of birth.
      </p>

      <p>
        Verifiable Credentials support <strong>selective disclosure</strong>: the ability to prove
        a specific claim without revealing the underlying data. Standards like
        <a href="https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-oauth-selective-disclosure-jwt-13.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SD-JWT</a>
        (Selective Disclosure JSON Web Tokens) enable this:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>"Is this person over 21?" → Yes/No — without revealing the actual date of birth</li>
        <li>"Is this person authorized to work in Costa Rica?" → Yes, credential issued by DGME — without revealing their immigration file number</li>
        <li>"Is this a real driver's license?" → Yes, issued by COSEVI, classes A and B — without revealing the license number or address</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The verifier gets exactly the information they need — cryptographically proven — and nothing
        more. There's nothing extra to store, nothing extra to breach.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Separating identity from finance</h2>

      <p>
        The Transak breach reveals another architectural flaw: the identity verification system
        (KYC) was connected to the financial system (crypto on-ramp). When the KYC vendor was
        breached, the attacker gained access to identity data because the financial service
        stored it.
      </p>

      <p>
        A well-designed system physically separates these layers:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The identity layer</strong> lives on the user's device. The user holds their
          credentials, signs consent with their identity key, and presents proofs to verifiers.
          No server stores their identity documents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The financial layer</strong> operates through a regulated custody provider.
          The user's financial account is managed by a licensed entity, but that entity never
          sees the user's identity documents — it only receives a cryptographic confirmation that
          KYC was completed.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Compromise one layer, and the other remains unaffected. An attacker who breaches the
        financial custody provider gets financial transaction data but zero identity documents.
        An attacker who somehow accesses the identity layer on one device gets one person's
        credentials (encrypted, biometric-locked) — not millions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The real cost of honeypots</h2>

      <p>
        When a credit card is stolen, the issuing bank bears the cost. The user gets a new card.
        Life goes on.
      </p>

      <p>
        When a passport photo is stolen, <strong>the user bears the cost</strong> — for the rest of
        their life. They can't get a new face. They can't change their date of birth. They can monitor
        their credit, freeze their accounts, and spend years dealing with the consequences of an
        identity they didn't lose but that was stolen from a company they were forced to trust.
      </p>

      <p>
        KYC regulations exist for good reasons: preventing money laundering, terrorist financing,
        and fraud. But the implementation — "upload your passport photo to our server" — creates
        a new class of risk that may be worse than the one it prevents. Every KYC database is a
        ticking time bomb. When it explodes, the shrapnel hits the users, not the company.
      </p>

      <p>
        Verifiable Credentials don't weaken KYC. They strengthen it — by proving identity
        cryptographically instead of storing evidence centrally. The verification is stronger
        (you can't Photoshop a cryptographic signature) and the risk is eliminated (there's
        nothing to breach).
      </p>

      <p>
        The choice is between a world where your passport photo sits on a hundred servers
        waiting to be stolen, and a world where it never leaves your pocket.
      </p>
    </section>

